/* ============================================================================
   derangements.epher -- permutations with no fixed point: round(n! / e)

   A derangement is a rearrangement in which nothing stays put: for a
   shuffled deck or a gift exchange, the number of arrangements where no
   card (or no guest) keeps its place. For n objects the count !n is the
   integer nearest n! / e, a classic result of de Montmort, and the
   script checks that rounding recipe against an exact recursion,

     !0 = 1,  !1 = 0,  !n = (n - 1) * (!(n - 1) + !(n - 2)),

   for n = 4 through 8. The values: 9, 44, 265, 1854, 14833. The share
   of all n! arrangements that are derangements tends to 1/e, about
   0.3679, whatever n is.

   Source: de Montmort (1708); Feller, "An Introduction to Probability
   Theory and Its Applications", volume 1, chapter 4. The recursion is
   exact; n! / e is a float, and rounding it stays safe because n! / e
   is never near a half-integer once n is past 2 (the error of the
   approximation is about 1/(n + 1)).

   Run it from a terminal:
     Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/probability/counting/derangements.epher
     Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\probability\counting\derangements.epher"
     macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/probability/counting/derangements.epher
   Web/desktop:             paste the file into the entry, press Enter

   The knob to edit is `nmax`; everything below it is machinery.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- the knob ----
const nmax = 8

// ---- helpers ----
def der(n) = if n <= 1 then 1 - n else (n - 1) * (der(n - 1) + der(n - 2))
// der(n) is the exact derangement count: !0 = 1, !1 = 0, and for n > 1
// a fixed-point-free permutation is built by sending n to one of n - 1
// other places and deranging the rest.

// ---- results ----
// n! / e and its nearest integer, for n = 4 to 8.
for n in 4 to nmax do fact(n) / exp(1)
for n in 4 to nmax do round(fact(n) / exp(1))

// The exact recursion agrees with the rounding recipe on every row.
for n in 4 to nmax do round(fact(n) / exp(1)) == der(n)

// The classic check: 9 derangements of 4 objects (the 24 arrangements
// of four cards include 9 that move every card).
print("derangements of 4 objects:", der(4))

// The limiting share 1/e of arrangements with no fixed point.
print("share that are derangements, for large n:", 1 / exp(1))
print("share for n = 8:", der(8) / fact(8))
/* ---- expected output ----
= 8
= {8.82910658811, 44.1455329406, 264.873197643, 1854.1123835, 14832.899068}
= {9, 44, 265, 1854, 14833}
= {true, true, true, true, true}
= derangements of 4 objects: 9
= share that are derangements, for large n: 0.367879441171
= share for n = 8: 0.367881944444
*/
